# KNOWN GAP (filed 2026-08-15, surfaced by the R42 Track-B EXECUTOR while
# probing residual comment shapes, orchestrator-verified): `auto`-typed DICT
# and SET comprehensions over a plain `Vector[int]` produce SILENT WRONG
# VALUES — `auto dc = {x: x * 10 for x in xs}` gives `dc[1]` == 3 (expected
# 10) and `auto sc = {x for x in xs}` gives `sc.len()` == 0 (expected 3).
# Both check clean and run without error — a wrong-code cell, worse than the
# filed resource-element comprehension CRASH family (that family's
# Vector[int] LIST cell is correct; these dict/set forms are not).
# The explicitly-typed range form CC-FAILs (a second cell of the same root).
# Likely root: the name-prefix element-type inference debt (the filed rule-2
# entry) mis-typing the comprehension's accumulator.
# INTENDED: dc[1] == 10, sc.len() == 3.
# Wired test: sound_auto_dict_set_comprehension_values (tests/integration.rs, #[ignore]d).
void main():
    Vector[int] xs = [1, 2, 3]
    auto dc = {x: x * 10 for x in xs}
    auto sc = {x for x in xs}
    print(dc[1])
    print(sc.len())
